Roughly 20 years of *hack culminated today with the final ascention to
complete all the classes.  Beginning way back with the old Hack,
stopping and starting the game over the years and to the modern game,
it is finally done.

I got the idea to ascend them all maybe 3 years ago when I tried the
annual tournament and it re-sparked my interest.  It's funny how the
game can be forgotten for years and years and then suddenly the old
addiction comes back.

The were four remaining classes I had barely ever played - Healer,
Caveman, Barbarian, and Rogue.  The last two were Barbarian & Rogue.
The Barbarian game was absurdly generous.  I found 3 bones files
(thanks to Hearse) all with nearly complete ascention kits.  Healer was
the most difficult.

On the Barbarian game -
I couldn't resist picking up the Magic Mirror of Merlin from a bones
pile.  I wished for a helm of opposite alignment so I could pick it up,
even though I knew I'd lose my godly protection.  Afterwords, I trekked
back up to murder the priest I'd bought protection from in the first
place, got the gold and then bought more protection in the Valley.
Then of course I murdered that priest just on principle.  Sometimes
playing a chaotic character can be fun.

I used Grayswandir, which I found just sitting on the ground, with
another silver sabre.  Strangely, I never got Cleaver.  When Rodney
came around I switched to Stormbringer to drain his life.  But I was
doing so much damage with two +7 weapons I didn't really need to use
that strategy.

Gmon the Slayer            St:25 Dx:18 Co:18 In:18 Wi:21 Ch:9  Chaotic
Astral Plane $:691 HP:610(610) Pw:91(91) AC:-40 Exp:23

The Rogue game was actually quite fun, I'm not sure why I avoided them.
 The quest is tricky and added a new dimension to the game.  I found
one good bones with Magicbane, which I didn't really use.  I stuck with
Stormbringer and another +7 Elven broadsword.  Then something odd
happened - I couldn't hit the shades on Orcus.  My weapons passed
harmlessly through them.  I don't remember that ever happening before.
I wished for a silver sabre and used it instead.  This game was also a
little too easy, as I found a full / of wishing early in the game.
With the key providing 1/2 damage and ~400hp I was never in much
danger.

It was also the first time I really used dagger-tossing as a main
strategy.  4 rustproof +7 daggers flying at an enemy is pretty lethal.


Since I was trying to complete the game I shamelessly wished, polypiled
and genocided away.  I left 'h' alone for the Rogue game since I feel
kind of guilty wiping out all hobbits and dwarves just to kill the
flayers.

 Some thoughts on the game in general -

 When I first started I played mostly Priests, because I thought
knowing the cursed status made the game easier.  What it taught me
mostly was to avoid direct fighting.

 When the new spell system entered the game I mostly ignored them,
until I finally started playing wizards and found them probably the
most fun of all.  My 2nd favorites are either Monk, Tourist or
Archeologist.  I discovered that I do not like playing Knights or
Rangers.  In fact I won't play Ranger at all again unless the arrow
patch is applied.  Having to ID arrows all the time was kind of
tedious.

 For Barbarians, the quest artifact is pretty useless.  I don't think
it even gives magic resistance.  One thing that might be nice to give
it is magic mapping ability.  It wouldn't give the Barbarian much
tactical advantage (since it doesn't need one), but would add a
convenience at least.

Other class thoughts -
Caveman, another thumper class.  The sceptre is intresting.

Valk was my first ascention, I believe.  The ability to toss/return
Mjollner is huge.

Archeologist - The two-handed Archeologist of Death is a lot of fun (+7
Grayswandir + +7 silver sabre + gauntlets of power.)  Nothing can stand
in your way, plus you get most of the good spells.

Samurai - Mmm, nothing stands out except getting cut in half in the
quest.

Tourist's quest is my favorite, and the PYEC is pretty nice too.

Wizard is crazy powerful with his Quest artifact.

Monk is perhaps the most balanced, and most fun.

Healer - key word is Patience.  Quest nemesis should be sleep
resistant.

Knight - At the end, I just didn't care about chivalry.  I don't pray
in the 2nd half of the game anyway.  I may have been a complete cad but
so what.  Guess I'm more the black knight than white.

Priest - I would like to see a weighted spellbook system, where if you
play priest then priestly spellbooks are more likely.  Just a thought.
A "bless" spell would be great.

Ideas for what's next -

Vegetarian, Wishless, genoless, polyless Wizard or Monk seems like the
most pure way to play the game.

Slash'em.  I have never even downloaded it, but I think I will now.

Woo hoo!!!

